  "seoDescription": "Choose a parameter-matched SwiGLU hidden width under an available-width limit, then evaluate the bias-free block.",
  "description": "Compute the forward pass of a parameter-matched SwiGLU feedforward MLP block using gated linear units and the Swish activation function.\n\n**Mathematical Formulation:**\n\n$$\n\\text{SwiGLU}(x) = \\left( \\text{Swish}(x W_{\\text{gate}}) \\odot (x W_{\\text{up}}) \\right) W_{\\text{down}}\n$$\n\n**Step-by-Step Implementation:**\n1. Project input activations through gate and up-projection weight matrices.\n2. Apply Swish non-linearity x / (1 + exp(-x)) to the gate activations.\n3. Multiply activated gate elementwise with up-projected activations.\n4. Down-project hidden representations back to model dimension.\n\n**Returns:** List of output activations with matching input feature dimensionality.",
  "examples": [
    "Input: x = [1.0, 2.0, 3.0]\nOutput: result matching Parameter-Matched SwiGLU Block specification."
  ],
  "requirements": "* Must implement `cs336_l03_parameter_matched_swiglu` using PYTORCH.\n* Must preserve input dimensions and return correct output types.\n* Vectorized and numerically stable.",
  "theory": "## Understanding the Theory: Parameter-Matched SwiGLU Feedforward Layer\n\n### Core Intuition: Why Do We Need This?\nCompute the forward pass of a parameter-matched SwiGLU feedforward MLP block using gated linear units and the Swish activation function. In modern deep learning and large-scale AI architectures, numerical correctness, memory efficiency, and deterministic computation form the foundational substrate for stable model training and fast inference. Understanding how each mathematical operator maps to low-level tensor algorithms enables practitioners to diagnose bottlenecks, eliminate silent numerical bugs, and design scalable custom operators.\n\n### Mathematical Principles & Formulation\nThe mathematical formulation governing this operation is given by:\n\n$$\n\\text{SwiGLU}(x) = \\left( \\text{Swish}(x W_{\\text{gate}}) \\odot (x W_{\\text{up}}) \\right) W_{\\text{down}}\n$$\n\nEach variable and term plays a precise functional role. In neural network training, preserving dimensional consistency across batch, sequence, and feature channels is essential. Numerical precision and dynamic range must be carefully preserved to avoid catastrophic floating-point cancellation, gradient underflow, or numerical instability.\n\n### Step-by-Step Computational Workflow\n1. **Phase 1: Input Validation & Dimension Setup**: Inspect tensor shapes, ensure contiguous memory layouts, and validate bounds.\n2. **Phase 2: Core Mathematical Transformation**: Project input activations through gate and up-projection weight matrices.\n3. **Phase 3: Iterative Execution & Reduction**: Apply Swish non-linearity x / (1 + exp(-x)) to the gate activations.\n4. **Phase 4: Output Assembly & Precision Guarantee**: Down-project hidden representations back to model dimension.\n\n### Practical Machine Learning Applications & Nuances\n* **Scalability & Performance**: Vectorized execution minimizes Python interpreter overhead and saturates modern SIMD and GPU hardware execution units.\n* **Numerical Stability**: Defensive handling of small denominators, extreme exponentials, and boundary tails prevents NaN propagation during large distributed training runs.\n* **Production Reliability**: Deterministic implementations guarantee reproducibility across different evaluation batches and distributed parallel ranks.",
